<PostLayout
  title="Creating a Captivating Documentation Homepage with reStructuredText"
  date="2021"
  summary="A reusable template for building well-structured documentation homepages using sphinx-design cards, tabs, and grids."
>

<p>
  A well-structured documentation homepage serves as the gateway to your product documentation.
  It must be easy to navigate and enable discoverability of your content. This post provides a
  template you can customize, built using the
  <a href="https://sphinx-design.readthedocs.io/en/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sphinx-design</a>
  extension.
</p>

<h2>Prerequisites</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Install sphinx-design: <code>pip install sphinx-design</code></li>
  <li>Add <code>'sphinx_design'</code> to your <code>conf.py</code> extensions list</li>
</ul>

<h2>The Template</h2>

<p>This template uses three sphinx-design elements: <strong>grids</strong> for product cards,
<strong>tabs</strong> for organizing documentation by section, and <strong>cards</strong> for
visual navigation.</p>

<pre><code>Welcome to Your Project Documentation Home
==========================================

Explore our guides and examples to integrate
Your Project across various solutions.

.. grid:: 2

    .. grid-item-card::  Your Project for Solution 1
       :link: https://yourproject.com/solution1

        Brief description of Solution 1.

    .. grid-item-card::  Your Project for Solution 2
       :link: https://yourproject.com/solution2

        Brief description of Solution 2.

Browse Documentation by Product
===============================

Product 1
---------

.. tab-set::

    .. tab-item:: Installation

        * `Installation Guide &lt;link&gt;`__
        * `Configuration Reference &lt;link&gt;`__

    .. tab-item:: API Reference

        * `API Overview &lt;link&gt;`__
        * `Endpoints Reference &lt;link&gt;`__</code></pre>

<h2>Why This Works</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Grid cards</strong> provide visual entry points for different product areas, making the homepage scannable</li>
  <li><strong>Tab sets</strong> organize dense link lists without overwhelming the page. Users can find their section without scrolling through everything</li>
  <li><strong>Consistent structure</strong> across product sections makes the homepage predictable and learnable</li>
</ul>

<h2>Design Principle</h2>

<p>
  The goal of a documentation homepage is not to display all your content. It is to help users
  quickly identify which section they need and get there in one click. Cards and tabs serve this
  purpose better than long lists of links or paragraphs of text.
</p>

